I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, and frozen analysis guidance first, then build and refine the phase model in GSAS-II. I’ll keep the final exports tied to the exact accepted project and run the required one-shot polish only after the model is terminal.

The sample is a WO₃–Mn₃O₄ reaction fired at 800 °C, so likely candidates include Mn tungstates plus residual/decomposition oxides, but I’ll let the peak families establish that. I’m now checking the frozen COD index interface, the GSAS-II export expectations, and the exact bounded-polish constraints before any model construction.

The strongest family is decisively wolframite MnWO₄: its 18.50°, 23.73°, 30.03/30.47°, and higher-angle reflections all recur with an approximately −0.25° displacement. A second coherent family matches monoclinic WO₃ (notably near 22.89°, 24.17°, 28.48°, and 33–34°). I’m branching the plausible WO₃ references under an identical first-stage refinement so the choice is based on the whole profile, not one peak.

The joint zero/height fit is mathematically unstable here (99.99% correlation, both terms driven to bounds), exactly the kind of calibration branch the supplied rules say to reject. I’m discarding that branch and will carry one tested angular correction at a time; the phase/cell evidence obtained before it remains usable.

